---
// Open data (#72): what the API publishes, the license, and how to cite it.
// Counts and links come from the same code that writes /api/v1/, so this page
// never promises a file the build did not make.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { catalog } from '../lib/api.mjs';
export const page = { door: "about", label: "Open data", summary: "Every listing as free JSON files for apps and AI tools, with license and how to cite" };
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repo = 'https://github.com/david-shortman/mon-county-community-hub';
const cat = await catalog();
const built = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', { dateStyle: 'long', timeZone: 'America/New_York' }).format(new Date(cat.generated));
const api = (p) => `${base}/api/v1/${p}`;
const LABELS = { orgs: 'Groups and offices', resources: 'Programs and services', calendar: 'Deadlines and dates', corrections: 'Corrections' };
---
<Base title="Open data for Morgantown and Mon County" description="Everything Morgantown's listings, dates and corrections as free JSON files that apps and AI tools can read, with the license and how to cite them.">
  <h1>Open data</h1>
  <p class="lede">Every listing on this site is also published as plain data files that apps, websites and AI tools can read. They are free to use. No key, no sign-up.</p>

  <h2>What is published</h2>
  <p>The files are in JSON, a plain text format that apps and AI tools can read. They are made from the same records as the site, so they always match what the site shows. They were last built on {built}, and the site rebuilds every morning.</p>
  <ul>
    {cat.types.map((t) => (
      <li><a href={api(`${t.name}.json`)}>{LABELS[t.name] ?? t.name}</a>: {t.count} records.</li>
    ))}
    <li><a href={api('index.json')}>The catalog</a>: every file, how many records each has, the license, and the build time.</li>
    <li><a href={api('openapi.yaml')}>The field list for developers</a> (OpenAPI 3.1): every field and what it means.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/calendar.ics`}>The calendar for calendar apps</a> (calendar.ics).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Developers can also get one record at a time, like <code>/api/v1/orgs/&#123;slug&#125;.json</code>. The catalog lists the address for each kind.</p>

  <h2>Every record shows its sources</h2>
  <p>Each record has a <code>sources</code> list. Every source has a web address and the date it was last checked. Please show both when you reuse a record, and send people to the source. Details change, so people should check the source before they act. A record whose oldest check is more than {cat.stale_after_days} days old may be out of date.</p>

  <h2>License and how to cite</h2>
  <p>The data is under <a href={cat.license.url}>Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a> (CC BY 4.0). You can use it for anything, even in a paid product. Credit "{cat.license.credit}" and link to <a href={cat.license.credit_url}>{cat.license.credit_url.replace(/^https:\/\//, '')}</a>. For example:</p>
  <blockquote><p>Listing from {cat.license.credit} ({cat.license.credit_url.replace(/^https:\/\//, '')}), CC BY 4.0. Source: the group's own page, checked on the date shown.</p></blockquote>
  <p>The code that builds the site is under the MIT License, on <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>.</p>

  <h2>Will the files change?</h2>
  <p>We only add fields to version 1. We never remove or rename one. If we ever need to, we will publish a new version at <code>/api/v2/</code> and keep version 1 up for at least 6 months. A check runs on every change to make sure of this.</p>
  <p>The files work from other websites too: GitHub Pages lets any site read them.</p>
</Base>
